prompt:
Use Three.js to implement ocean surface rendering based on the superposition of multiple sine waves and Gerstner waves, including reflection, refraction, and foam effects.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Water Surface Geometry]
(1) Water surface plane: PlaneGeometry(1000, 1000, 256, 256), y=0, placed horizontally;
(2) Use a custom ShaderMaterial (vertexShader + fragmentShader);
(3) Pass uniforms including time (animation time), windDir, waveHeight, waveFreq, choppiness, waterColor, sunDirection, etc.;

[Wave Generation - Vertex Shader]
(4) Implement the superposition of at least 8 sine waves, with each wave having an independent direction, amplitude, frequency, phase, and speed;
(5) Calculate the parameters of the 8 waves in JavaScript based on the wind direction (windDir) and global parameters (waveHeight, waveFreq), then pass them to the shader;
(6) Distribute each wave's direction around the wind direction (with a random offset within ±60°), and attenuate amplitude and frequency in a cascade (high-frequency waves have small amplitudes);
(7) Gerstner wave displacement formulas (to give the water surface sharp peaks):
  - dx = -choppiness * amplitude * sin(dot(direction, position.xz) * frequency + time * speed)
  - dy = amplitude * cos(dot(direction, position.xz) * frequency + time * speed)
  - Superimpose all waves to obtain the final displacement;
(8) Normal recalculation: compute differences in the x and z directions of the displacement field (or use analytical derivatives) to obtain the normal vector for each fragment;

[Water Surface Shading - Fragment Shader]
(9) Reflection: use an environment cube map (a simple gradient-sky CubeTexture may be generated inline, or PMREMGenerator may be used to generate one from sky colors) to sample the reflection color in the water surface normal direction;
(10) Refraction: sample the underwater color based on UV coordinates offset by the normal (simplified as a dark gradient);
(11) Fresnel effect: fresnel = pow(1.0 - max(dot(viewDir, normal), 0.0), 5.0), blending reflection and refraction;
(12) Foam: overlay white foam at wave crests (where the y displacement exceeds a threshold or the Jacobian determinant < a threshold);
(13) Sun specular highlight: Blinn-Phong or GGX specular highlight, with its direction aligned with the sun position;
(14) Final color = mix(refraction, reflection, fresnel) + specular + foam;

[Control Panel]
(15) Wind-direction slider id="windDir", range 0-360, step 1, default 45;
(16) Wave-height slider id="waveHeight", range 0.1-3.0, step 0.1, default 1.0;
(17) Wave-frequency slider id="waveFreq", range 0.5-5.0, step 0.1, default 1.0;
(18) Choppiness slider id="choppiness", range 0-2.0, step 0.1, default 1.0;
(19) Water-color picker id="waterColor" (input type=color), default #001e0f;

[Sun and Sky]
(20) Simple sky background (gradient or simple skybox);
(21) Use DirectionalLight to simulate sunlight;

[State Exposure (Required)]
(22) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - windDir: number (wind direction in degrees)
  - waveHeight: number (wave-height parameter value)
  - waveFreq: number (wave-frequency parameter value)
  - choppiness: number (choppiness)
  - waterColor: string (water color in hexadecimal)
  - waveCount: number (number of waves, must be ≥ 8)
  - simTime: number (simulation time in seconds)
  - maxDisplacement: number (the absolute value of the maximum water-surface y displacement in the current frame, calculated on the JavaScript side by iterating over the geometry vertices or passed back from the vertex shader)

Output a single HTML file that runs when opened directly in a browser.
